@@ -0,0 +1,118 @@
+# Kali & Security Pentesting - Copy-Ready Spec for Integration with Free Claude Code
+
+## OVERVIEW
+- **Purpose**: Provide a compact, ethical, and secure pentesting capability that integrates with the Free Claude Code terminal and file explorer while avoiding operational risk and legal exposure.
+- **Scope**: reconnaissance, vulnerability discovery, safe exploitation in isolated sandboxes, reporting, remediation guidance, and handoff to defenders.
+- **Constraints**: No unauthorized testing; all actions require signed Rules of Engagement (RoE) and explicit scope; high‑risk actions must be sandboxed and audited.
+
+## LEGAL & ETHICAL RULES
+- **Authorization**: Obtain written permission specifying targets, time windows, and allowed techniques.
+- **Rules of Engagement**: Define scope, allowed tools, escalation path, data handling, and kill-switch conditions.
+- **Privacy**: Avoid exfiltrating personal data; mask or redact PII in reports.
+- **Disclosure**: Follow coordinated disclosure timelines; include remediation windows and contact points.
+- **Audit Trail**: Log every action with user_id, timestamp, command, target, sandbox_id, and SHA256 anchors.
+
+## ENVIRONMENT & ISOLATION
+- **Execution model**:
+  • Local analysis: static scanning, parsing, linting, and metadata extraction in the main IDE.
+  • Dynamic testing: run only inside ephemeral microVMs or containers (Firecracker, gVisor, or nsjail).
+  • Network isolation: sandboxed VMs use controlled virtual networks with simulated services or monitored egress.
+- **Artifact handling**:
+  • All suspicious binaries saved to quarantine store with SHA256, size, and provenance metadata.
+  • Raw artifacts never shown in main UI; only sanitized summaries and disassembly views in Safe IDE.
+- **Kill-switch (Yosemite Sam)**:
+  • Automatic triggers: unexpected network egress, sandbox escape indicators, policy violations.
+  • On kill: snapshot metadata, terminate processes, wipe ephemeral FS, create incident ticket.
+
+## TOOL CATEGORIES (HIGH-LEVEL)
+- Reconnaissance: passive discovery, DNS, certificate transparency, public metadata aggregation.
+- Scanning: port/service discovery and fingerprinting (non-invasive modes only by default).
+- Web testing: proxying and analysis (intercept in sandboxed proxy with request/response logging).
+- Static analysis: AST/tree-sitter parsing, dependency analysis, secret scanning.
+- Dynamic analysis: behavior-only dry runs in quarantined sandboxes; no uncontrolled execution on host.
+- Exploit frameworks: only in isolated lab environments with explicit authorization; results summarized, not raw payloads.
+- Reporting & triage: automated findings export, severity scoring, remediation steps, and reproducible test artifacts.
+
+## SAFE WORKFLOWS (EXAMPLE PATTERNS)
+- Discovery → Validate → Isolate → Dry Run → Report
+  1. Discovery: collect metadata and surface potential issues (no active exploitation).
+  2. Validate: run static checks and lightweight probes.
+  3. Isolate: if active testing needed, snapshot code/artifact and spawn sandbox.
+  4. Dry Run: behavior-only execution with network simulation and monitoring.
+  5. Report: produce JSONC + MD summary with remediation guidance and reproducible test artifacts.
+- Human-in-the-loop: require explicit confirmation for any action that escalates from analysis to execution.
+
+## INTEGRATION WITH IDE & TERMINAL
+- Single-click selection: mark file/folder as current context for agents; attach path + SHA256 + metadata.
+- Double-click: auto-cd into project; sync agent working directory.
+- Right-click actions (file-type aware):
+  • Static Scan, Run Linter, Send to Security Agent, Open in Safe IDE, Quarantine, Dry Run in Sandbox, Extract IOCs, Create Incident.
+- Explorer badges: color-coded (error, warning, threat) with symbols and hover metadata.
+- Context menus produce declarative action objects (id, label, required_permissions, handler_endpoint, audit_level).
+- All security actions route through AID → SSD → DSD → PED → TID pipeline with policy checks.
+
+## AUDIT, LOGGING & EVIDENCE
+- Immutable logs: append-only records for every action (user, command, target, sandbox_id, sha256, result).
+- Evidence packaging: JSONC bundle with sanitized logs, MD summary, and artifact references (not raw payloads).
+- Retention & access: RBAC for evidence stores; high-audit actions require multi-party approval to access raw artifacts.
+
+## REPORTING & DELIVERABLES
+- Standard report sections:
+  1. Executive summary (impact, severity, remediation priority)
+  2. Scope & RoE (what was tested, dates, authorizations)
+  3. Findings (id, severity, description, affected assets)
+  4. Repro steps (high-level, non-actionable; link to sandbox snapshot)
+  5. Evidence (sanitized logs, SHA256 anchors, screenshots)
+  6. Remediation guidance (concrete fixes, config changes, detection rules)
+  7. Timeline & audit trail
+- Machine-friendly output: JSONC with fields: finding_id, severity, file_path, sha256, line_refs[], suggested_fix[], sandbox_snapshot_id.
+- Human-friendly output: Markdown with badges and action links (Open in Safe IDE, Create Ticket).
+
+## SECURITY METRICS & DETECTION RULES
+- Risk scoring: combine CVSS-like severity, exploitability, and business impact.
+- Detection rules: translate findings into SIEM-friendly alerts and YARA-like signatures where applicable.
+- Continuous monitoring: schedule periodic re-scans and regression checks in CI with safe, non-invasive probes.
+
+## CI/CD & AUTOMATION (SAFE)
+- Pre-commit hooks: static secret scanning and linting (local, fast).
+- CI jobs: run static analysis and dependency checks; dynamic tests only in isolated test clusters with explicit gating.
+- Auto-ticketing: create remediation tickets with reproducible evidence bundles; require human triage for high-risk items.
+
+## TRAINING & RED/BLUE OPERATIONS
+- Training labs: maintain isolated lab environments for exploit development and validation.
+- Red/Blue separation: red team runs in lab; blue team receives sanitized evidence and detection guidance.
+- Tabletop exercises: use sanitized incident artifacts to rehearse response without exposing raw payloads.
+
+## SECURITY HARDENING FOR THE IDE
+- Extension policy: signed extensions only; sandboxed extension runtime; permission model for network, FS, and agent access.
+- Least privilege: IDE processes run with minimal privileges; sandbox runners use ephemeral credentials.
+- Telemetry: opt-in, anonymized telemetry; sensitive events require explicit consent and stronger access controls.
+
+## SAMPLE JSONC FINDING (MACHINE)
+{
+  "finding_id":"F-20260824-001",
+  "severity":"high",
+  "title":"Insecure deserialization in module X",
+  "file":"file:///project/src/module.py",
+  "line_refs":[128,130],
+  "sha256":"<sha256>",
+  "sandbox_snapshot":"snapshot-042",
+  "suggested_fix":["validate input types","use safe deserialization library"],
+  "audit":{"user":"analyst@org","timestamp":"2026-08-24T19:11:00Z"}
+}
+
+## SAMPLE MARKDOWN SUMMARY (HUMAN)
+### Finding F-20260824-001 — Insecure deserialization ❌
+**Severity:** High
+**File:** `src/module.py` lines 128–130
+**Summary:** Unsafe deserialization of untrusted input may allow remote code execution.
+**Suggested Fix:** Validate input types and use a safe deserialization library.
+**Evidence:** sandbox snapshot `snapshot-042` (SHA256: `<sha256>`).
+**Actions:** [Open in Safe IDE] [Create Incident] [Quarantine Artifact]
+
+## IMPLEMENTATION NOTES & BEST PRACTICES
+- Default to non-destructive analysis; require explicit opt-in for active testing.
+- Keep heavy analysis off the main UI; delegate to background agents and daemons.
+- Use microVMs for dynamic tests; never run untrusted code on host.
+- Provide clear UX for consent, confirmations, and audit visibility.
+- Automate packaging of sanitized evidence for defenders; never auto-apply fixes.
